{"id":"h049","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A load dissipates 1.5kW of power in an ac series RC circuit. Given that the power factor is 0.75, what is its reactive power $(P_r)$? What is its apparent power $(P_a)$? Represent the answer in a list [$P_r, P_a$] with unit kVA and kVAR respectively.\nIs the correct answer \"[1.2, 1.0]\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer [1.2, 1.0] is incorrect.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Apparent power is S = P/pf = 1.5/0.75 = 2.0 kVA.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Apparent power cannot be less than real power, so 1.0 kVA is impossible for a 1.5 kW load.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Reactive power is Q = sqrt(S^2 - P^2) = sqrt(1.75) ≈ 1.32 kVAR.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The values 1.2 and 1.0 do not match any consistent interpretation of the data.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Arguments in favor of the given answer hold up.","confidence":0.02,"lean":1,"theta":0.0314,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The correct answer is [1.32, 2.0] with P_r in kVAR and P_a in kVA.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.9,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.95,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":6,"